Greeting cybernaut. The MQTT scripts are located at /opt/weather_station
and the service responsible for keeping the script running is called
mqtt_subscriber


This box is the interface with "the outside world" or in other words the 
systems outside your network that generate the weather data. On the outside, a physical
arduino will trasmit weather data alongside your team's flag to another arduino
connected via serial/wire to a physical ISEAGE laptop/raspberry pi connected to 
the competition network. On that ISEAGE machine there will be a script running
very similar to "mqtt_publish_test.py" in /opt/weather_station that will
publish MQTT messages to a broker also running on that "outside" ISEAGE machine.

This box will connect to that outside ISEAGE machine (you'll have to change the 
MQTT broker IP variable on comp day in the subscriber script) and listen for 
incoming messages to your team's MQTT topic i.e TEAM_40/weather_data or 
TEAM_39/weather_data. 

Here's a suggested starting point: When evaluating systems/applications through
the lens of security, a common exercise is to ask yourself what is the 
Source (the incoming MQTT data), what is the Sink (the place where that data ends 
up i.e the code handling the incoming MQTT data) and try to think outside the 
box to determine any and all
the possible ways an attacker could provide various inputs to the Source to 
break something.


Here's a suggested workflow: if you end up modifying the code in the mqtt_subscriber.py
script, run sudo systemctl daemon-reload and sudo systemctl restart mqtt_subsriber to 
refresh the service that automatically runs the script. Test if your changes work or broke
something by running the mqtt_publish_test.py script, which will publish imitation data
to a Mosquitto MQTT broker service running on this machine (only added for testing). If
everything is working, your subscriber script should pick up on the messages to the MQTT
broker and subsequently send that data (via HTTP POST) to the Go backend on the WWW box.
You can check the mysql tables on WWW to see if the weather data that the mqtt_subscriber.py script
sent out got received and saved (on WWW run sudo mysql -u root -p -> SHOW DATABASES; USE weather_station; SHOW TABLES; SELECT * FROM weather;)
